"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,040\nThe Learning Brain.\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,525\nEpisode three.\n\n3\n00:00:07,545 --> 00:00:08,906\nPriming.\n\n4\n00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:12,887\nRead this word.\n\n5\n00:00:12,907 --> 00:00:13,615\nDoctor.\n\n6\n00:00:13,796 --> 00:00:14,904\nNow this one.\n\n7\n00:00:15,097 --> 00:00:15,883\nNurse.\n\n8\n00:00:16,125 --> 00:00:17,519\nIn a classic experiment,\n\n9\n00:00:17,539 --> 00:00:24,013\npeople judged related pairs like these about eighty-five milliseconds faster than unrelated ones.\n\n10\n00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:26,545\nThat head start is called priming.\n\n11\n00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:28,657\nHere's one leading explanation.\n\n12\n00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:31,276\nYour knowledge isn't stored as a list.\n\n13\n00:00:31,522 --> 00:00:32,153\nIt's a web,\n\n14\n00:00:32,173 --> 00:00:34,202\nwhere related ideas are linked.\n\n15\n00:00:34,222 --> 00:00:34,497\nActivate one,\n\n16\n00:00:34,731 --> 00:00:37,260\nand some of that activity spreads to its neighbors.\n\n17\n00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:38,541\nThey're not fully on,\n\n18\n00:00:38,812 --> 00:00:41,724\nbut they're closer to the threshold.\n\n19\n00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:44,147\nSo when one of them actually arrives,\n\n20\n00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:45,982\nit takes less to tip it over.\n\n21\n00:00:46,052 --> 00:00:49,482\nThe same thing happens when you simply meet something twice.\n\n22\n00:00:49,781 --> 00:00:50,926\nThe second time,\n\n23\n00:00:51,025 --> 00:00:53,625\nthe brain does less work for the same result.\n\n24\n00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:55,510\nIn brain scans,\n\n25\n00:00:55,555 --> 00:00:58,474\nthe response to a repeated item is smaller.\n\n26\n00:00:58,745 --> 00:01:02,428\nAnd this can happen even when you don't remember the first encounter.\n\n27\n00:01:02,498 --> 00:01:04,240\nNow apply that to learning.\n\n28\n00:01:04,478 --> 00:01:05,191\nIn one study,\n\n29\n00:01:05,211 --> 00:01:08,140\npeople heard a vague paragraph about a procedure.\n\n30\n00:01:08,317 --> 00:01:09,913\nSort things into groups.\n\n31\n00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:11,857\nGo somewhere else if you have to.\n\n32\n00:01:12,105 --> 00:01:14,481\nOne group was told the topic first.\n\n33\n00:01:14,623 --> 00:01:16,080\nWashing clothes.\n\n34\n00:01:16,187 --> 00:01:18,221\nThey recalled about twice as much.\n\n35\n00:01:18,424 --> 00:01:19,804\nTold the topic afterward,\n\n36\n00:01:19,824 --> 00:01:21,293\nit didn't help at all.\n\n37\n00:01:21,363 --> 00:01:23,087\nThe words were identical.\n\n38\n00:01:23,364 --> 00:01:26,970\nWhat changed was what was already active when they arrived.\n\n39\n00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:30,132\nNew information needs something to attach to,\n\n40\n00:01:30,232 --> 00:01:32,725\nand it has to be switched on in advance.\n\n41\n00:01:32,795 --> 00:01:33,917\nOne caution.\n\n42\n00:01:34,223 --> 00:01:35,330\nThe dramatic claims,\n\n43\n00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:38,299\nthat a few stray words can steer your behavior,\n\n44\n00:01:38,351 --> 00:01:39,786\nhave not held up well.\n\n45\n00:01:40,131 --> 00:01:42,425\nThe effects that hold are these small,\n\n46\n00:01:42,445 --> 00:01:43,531\nspecific ones.\n\n47\n00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:45,269\nSo when you teach something new,\n\n48\n00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:46,335\nprime it first.\n\n49\n00:01:46,654 --> 00:01:48,977\nShow students where the session is headed.\n\n50\n00:01:49,228 --> 00:01:51,777\nAsk them to recall what they already know.\n\n51\n00:01:52,023 --> 00:01:54,706\nEven pose questions they can't answer yet.\n\n52\n00:01:55,096 --> 00:01:56,202\nIn experiments,\n\n53\n00:01:56,222 --> 00:02:00,198\na wrong guess before reading still beat extra study time.\n\n54\n00:02:00,268 --> 00:02:02,338\nPriming won't do the learning for you.\n\n55\n00:02:02,688 --> 00:02:06,815\nIt just means the new idea lands on a network that's already warm.\n"
